Uhh!

1516
01:07:13,530 --> 01:07:15,265
Who are you?

1517
01:07:19,237 --> 01:07:20,237
Mannix!

1518
01:07:20,270 --> 01:07:21,538
[Humming action music]

1519
01:07:21,572 --> 01:07:24,175
Let go, you fools!

1520
01:07:24,208 --> 01:07:26,210
Tell your men
to drop their arms.

1521
01:07:26,243 --> 01:07:28,078
Quickly! Don't move,
any of you.

1522
01:07:28,112 --> 01:07:31,115
It could be a trick.
He didn't say Simon says.

1523
01:07:31,148 --> 01:07:32,950
I am Omar pasha.

1524
01:07:32,983 --> 01:07:35,518
And I'm nayland Smith
of Scotland yard.

1525
01:07:35,552 --> 01:07:38,788
I thought I could
smell a policeman.

1526
01:07:38,822 --> 01:07:40,892
It seems that for once,

1527
01:07:40,925 --> 01:07:42,493
you and I could be
on the same side.

1528
01:07:42,527 --> 01:07:43,627
That would be fun.

1529
01:07:43,660 --> 01:07:45,328
I, too, seek fu manchu.

1530
01:07:45,362 --> 01:07:47,532
Well, gesundheit.

1531
01:07:47,565 --> 01:07:49,065
♪♪

1532
01:07:49,100 --> 01:07:52,537
We now return to Burt young
in "the Mickey Rooney story."

1533
01:07:52,570 --> 01:07:54,638
[Humming]

1534
01:07:56,040 --> 01:07:57,542
I'm sorry
I locked us in, okay?

1535
01:07:57,575 --> 01:08:01,712
Look, I'm here too.
I'm not happy about it, either!

1536
01:08:01,745 --> 01:08:03,680
You have done well,
Dr. Kessler.

1537
01:08:06,217 --> 01:08:09,120
His recovery
will take a long time.

1538
01:08:09,153 --> 01:08:11,888
We cannot afford to wait
for complete recovery.

1539
01:08:11,922 --> 01:08:13,223
It's real pricey.

1540
01:08:13,257 --> 01:08:14,858
As soon as he regains
consciousness,

1541
01:08:14,892 --> 01:08:16,227
he will be made to talk.

1542
01:08:16,260 --> 01:08:18,795
And if heracles refuses, hmm?

1543
01:08:18,828 --> 01:08:20,097
Hmm?

1544
01:08:20,130 --> 01:08:21,865
Need I remind you, doctor,

1545
01:08:21,898 --> 01:08:24,635
that he has
no alternative.

1546
01:08:24,668 --> 01:08:26,837
Oh, thanks.
The new light helps.

1547
01:08:26,870 --> 01:08:30,340
And if I refuse to help you
any further, hmm?

1548
01:08:30,373 --> 01:08:32,910
Then you will sign
this lady's death warrant.

1549
01:08:32,943 --> 01:08:34,378
Sure.
Got a pen?

1550
01:08:34,412 --> 01:08:37,582
Your help during the operation
was invaluable.

1551
01:08:40,050 --> 01:08:41,185
Ooh.

1552
01:08:41,218 --> 01:08:43,020
We have no
further use for you.

1553
01:08:44,388 --> 01:08:46,523
Oh! No...

1554
01:08:46,557 --> 01:08:48,625
Oh! Please, no!

1555
01:08:51,128 --> 01:08:53,797
I amuse me.
Ha ha.

1556
01:08:55,732 --> 01:08:57,802
Look after your patient,
doctor.

1557
01:08:57,835 --> 01:08:58,968
Please.

1558
01:08:59,002 --> 01:09:00,637
Watch him well.

1559
01:09:03,907 --> 01:09:06,877
Tonight on a very violent
"medical center"...

1560
01:09:06,910 --> 01:09:08,478
Curt!

1561
01:09:08,512 --> 01:09:10,413
Wait, wait,
you guys got me all wrong.

1562
01:09:10,447 --> 01:09:12,650
I'm a veterinary technician!

1563
01:09:14,785 --> 01:09:16,120
Father.

1564
01:09:16,153 --> 01:09:17,320
"That girl."

1565
01:09:17,353 --> 01:09:19,423
...have caught a spy.
Omar pasha.

1566
01:09:19,457 --> 01:09:21,558
Alone?

1567
01:09:21,592 --> 01:09:22,993
Yes, and unarmed,

1568
01:09:23,027 --> 01:09:25,128
as if he
invited capture.

1569
01:09:25,162 --> 01:09:27,297
Anything to end the pain.

1570
01:09:27,330 --> 01:09:28,765
Have the girl Lisa
brought to me.

1571
01:09:28,798 --> 01:09:30,300
Yes, father.

1572
01:09:31,902 --> 01:09:35,572
[Singing indistinctly]

1573
01:09:35,605 --> 01:09:39,377
I've got a crazy idea,
but it just might--

1574
01:09:39,410 --> 01:09:40,777
oh, forget it.

1575
01:09:45,115 --> 01:09:47,383
Let me outta here!

1576
01:09:48,885 --> 01:09:50,955
Let us outta here.

1577
01:09:52,690 --> 01:09:55,625
Oh, it's Paloma Picasso,
right where she belongs.

1578
01:09:55,658 --> 01:09:57,160
Who are you?

1579
01:09:57,193 --> 01:09:58,362
Anjelica huston.

1580
01:09:58,395 --> 01:09:59,330
Lisa.

1581
01:10:03,433 --> 01:10:04,935
Did they do that to you?

1582
01:10:04,968 --> 01:10:06,303
No, she thought
it would be cute.

1583
01:10:06,337 --> 01:10:07,938
Of course they did it.

1584
01:10:09,573 --> 01:10:11,775
This movie
is taking its toll on me.

1585
01:10:11,808 --> 01:10:13,477
Ohh... me too.

1586
01:10:13,510 --> 01:10:14,912
Let's get out of here.

1587
01:10:14,945 --> 01:10:16,213
Uhh!

1588
01:10:16,247 --> 01:10:20,250
I think this is the worst movie
they've ever made us do.

1589
01:10:23,020 --> 01:10:25,055
[Whooshing]

1590
01:10:25,088 --> 01:10:28,558
[Thud, creak, zoom]

1591
01:10:32,897 --> 01:10:34,298
[Whimpering]

1592
01:10:34,332 --> 01:10:36,167
Wow.

1593
01:10:36,200 --> 01:10:38,335
That one really stung.

1594
01:10:38,368 --> 01:10:39,570
Yeah.

1595
01:10:39,603 --> 01:10:41,138
It's a tough one, isn't it?

1596
01:10:41,172 --> 01:10:42,673
Yeah, whatever.

1597
01:10:42,707 --> 01:10:44,007
Boy, I'd hate
to meet this movie

1598
01:10:44,040 --> 01:10:46,310
in a dark alley
some night, huh?

1599
01:10:46,343 --> 01:10:47,678
- Yeah.
- Sure, whatever.

1600
01:10:47,712 --> 01:10:50,113
Hey, um, Tom,
you got a little--

1601
01:10:50,147 --> 01:10:51,415
don't, no, don't.

1602
01:10:51,448 --> 01:10:52,650
No, really,
you got a little--

1603
01:10:52,683 --> 01:10:53,983
no, just stop it, okay?
Just stop it!

1604
01:10:54,017 --> 01:10:55,918
[Whimpering]
Oh, see, this is terrible.

1605
01:10:55,953 --> 01:10:57,655
[Muttering incoherently]

1606
01:10:57,688 --> 01:10:59,122
Jeepers.

1607
01:10:59,155 --> 01:11:01,958
Yo, what's the deal
with fu manchu anyways?

1608
01:11:01,992 --> 01:11:03,793
It's not like
he's really evil.

1609
01:11:03,827 --> 01:11:05,162
He's just dull!

1610
01:11:05,195 --> 01:11:07,765
He's like some twisted
bureaucrat in silk pajamas.

1611
01:11:07,798 --> 01:11:09,032
[Whimpers]
It's terrible!

1612
01:11:09,065 --> 01:11:11,968
I'm really glad you brought
that up, crow,

1613
01:11:12,002 --> 01:11:13,437
because I happen
to have some artist--

1614
01:11:13,470 --> 01:11:15,072
[both]
No! No!

1615
01:11:15,105 --> 01:11:16,773
Oh, come on.

1616
01:11:16,807 --> 01:11:19,242
Come on, it'll add
a little levity, okay?

1617
01:11:19,275 --> 01:11:22,445
This is the story of fu manchu,
okay?

1618
01:11:22,478 --> 01:11:23,480
I don't care.

1619
01:11:23,513 --> 01:11:25,348
Fu manchu was born
Carl Ivan chu

1620
01:11:25,382 --> 01:11:28,818
in schenectady,
New York, in 1916.

1621
01:11:28,852 --> 01:11:31,322
His parents, David Wayne
and Patricia Ann chu,

1622
01:11:31,355 --> 01:11:33,857
owned the superior skate
and blade company,

1623
01:11:33,890 --> 01:11:37,828
a thriving ice skate
and cutlery business in 1931.

1624
01:11:37,862 --> 01:11:39,162
- Not cutlery!
- Yeah.

1625
01:11:39,195 --> 01:11:40,963
Young Carl's father
became very ill,

1626
01:11:40,998 --> 01:11:42,667
and they sold the business
and moved the family

1627
01:11:42,700 --> 01:11:46,803
to appleton, Wisconsin,
a move that broke his heart.

1628
01:11:46,837 --> 01:11:48,038
- Ohh.
- Broke my heart too!

1629
01:11:48,072 --> 01:11:49,973
It gets better, though.
It gets better.

1630
01:11:50,007 --> 01:11:52,877
Okay... now, okay,
a short year later,

1631
01:11:52,910 --> 01:11:55,812
ignoring the pleas
of his heartbroken family,

1632
01:11:55,845 --> 01:11:57,948
he moved by himself
to New York City,

1633
01:11:57,982 --> 01:11:59,215
the big apple.

1634
01:11:59,248 --> 01:12:01,185
His dream: To make it
on Broadway,

1635
01:12:01,218 --> 01:12:02,318
the great white way.

1636
01:12:02,352 --> 01:12:03,487
Was his dream shattered?

1637
01:12:03,520 --> 01:12:05,255
You copied these out
of "the new yorker."

1638
01:12:05,288 --> 01:12:07,490
No. He began his career
as most young hopefuls do,

1639
01:12:07,523 --> 01:12:09,693
running cigarettes
for Agnes demille.

1640
01:12:09,727 --> 01:12:10,960
[Both]
Agnes demille!

1641
01:12:10,993 --> 01:12:12,062
- Right.
- Oh, no!

1642
01:12:12,095 --> 01:12:14,063
This kept him busy
for 12 hours a day

1643
01:12:14,097 --> 01:12:15,465
and nearly
broke his spirit.

1644
01:12:15,498 --> 01:12:17,533
It was on one of his many trips
to the store

1645
01:12:17,567 --> 01:12:19,168
for a carton of camels
that he hit upon

1646
01:12:19,202 --> 01:12:21,038
what was to become
his famous stage name.

1647
01:12:21,072 --> 01:12:24,708
The story goes that a panhandler
asked him for a nickel to,

1648
01:12:24,742 --> 01:12:27,643
- "get some food, man."
- [Both] Oh, no!

1649
01:12:27,677 --> 01:12:29,913
Well, Carl liked that,
and that's all it took.

1650
01:12:29,947 --> 01:12:34,017
His name was fu manchu.
Okay?

1651
01:12:34,050 --> 01:12:35,885
After a brief stint
running sandwiches

1652
01:12:35,918 --> 01:12:37,520
for fatty arbuckle,
he got a job

1653
01:12:37,553 --> 01:12:38,822
sweeping the streets
in a clown outfit.

1654
01:12:38,855 --> 01:12:40,457
- Not a clown outfit!
- Yep.

1655
01:12:40,490 --> 01:12:41,792
- [Both protesting]
- Okay.

1656
01:12:41,825 --> 01:12:43,527
No, clowns are fun.

1657
01:12:43,560 --> 01:12:45,128
Okay, the idea caught,
and soon,

1658
01:12:45,162 --> 01:12:46,997
fu manchu was managing
over a dozen

1659
01:12:47,030 --> 01:12:48,365
clown street sweepers.

1660
01:12:48,398 --> 01:12:51,235
He was now in a position
to return to his first love,

1661
01:12:51,268 --> 01:12:52,568
dinner theater.

1662
01:12:52,602 --> 01:12:53,803
I'm going to be sick.

1663
01:12:53,837 --> 01:12:55,405
Okay, here we go.

1664
01:12:55,438 --> 01:12:57,808
His first role
was as the lovable Betty

1665
01:12:57,842 --> 01:13:00,610
in "let's drink a toast
to crazy legs,"

1666
01:13:00,643 --> 01:13:03,880
a short-lived,
off-Broadway musical

1667
01:13:03,913 --> 01:13:06,350
starring Danny Thomas
and Charles Nelson--

1668
01:13:06,383 --> 01:13:07,785
no! No! No!

1669
01:13:07,818 --> 01:13:10,620
Oh, what's the use?
Why am I up here?

1670
01:13:10,653 --> 01:13:12,857
What are you doing to us?

1671
01:13:12,890 --> 01:13:14,157
[All sobbing]

1672
01:13:14,190 --> 01:13:16,093
Auntie em!
Auntie em!

1673
01:13:16,127 --> 01:13:18,295
[Cackling]

1674
01:13:18,328 --> 01:13:19,463
Way to go, doc.

1675
01:13:19,497 --> 01:13:21,932
[Both]
Whoo!

1676
01:13:21,965 --> 01:13:23,400
Yes.

1677
01:13:27,037 --> 01:13:30,007
Well, we finally got you
on the run, eh, boobie?

1678
01:13:30,040 --> 01:13:32,508
Yeah, frank, there's some pie
in the kitchen.

1679
01:13:32,542 --> 01:13:35,412
Why don't you go slice us up
a couple of nice, big slabs

1680
01:13:35,445 --> 01:13:36,947
of French silk, huh?

1681
01:13:36,980 --> 01:13:37,948
And don't skimp.

1682
01:13:37,982 --> 01:13:40,250
Yes, sir!

1683
01:13:40,283 --> 01:13:42,752
As for you,
macaulay culkin,

1684
01:13:42,787 --> 01:13:44,153
get back into that theater!

1685
01:13:44,187 --> 01:13:47,925
Uh, after this message,
of course.

1686
01:13:47,958 --> 01:13:51,262
♪♪

1687
01:13:55,698 --> 01:13:56,733
[Whimpering]

1688
01:13:56,767 --> 01:13:58,602
Oh, man, I don't know.

1689
01:13:58,635 --> 01:13:59,737
Yeah.

1690
01:13:59,770 --> 01:14:00,803
Gonna make it?

1691
01:14:00,837 --> 01:14:02,840
[Whimpering]

1692
01:14:02,873 --> 01:14:05,775
He grabbed the girl and turned
her into a guy in a Fez.

1693
01:14:05,808 --> 01:14:08,012
He's been eating spaghetti.

1694
01:14:08,045 --> 01:14:09,680
Ew.

1695
01:14:09,713 --> 01:14:10,780
Hume cronyn.

1696
01:14:10,813 --> 01:14:13,617
So you didn't
call a cab then, huh?

1697
01:14:13,650 --> 01:14:15,118
Is this the way...

1698
01:14:15,152 --> 01:14:17,120
To San Jose.

1699
01:14:17,153 --> 01:14:19,957
...stranger in your house.

1700
01:14:19,990 --> 01:14:22,058
You are no stranger to me.

1701
01:14:22,092 --> 01:14:24,962
Only a friend
I haven't met yet.

1702
01:14:24,995 --> 01:14:27,832
But I never thought you
to be a fool.

1703
01:14:27,865 --> 01:14:30,033
Fools do not trade...

1704
01:14:30,067 --> 01:14:31,368
Fools rush in.

1705
01:14:31,402 --> 01:14:32,402
...lives.

1706
01:14:37,240 --> 01:14:39,408
Where's she been
all my death?

1707
01:14:41,378 --> 01:14:43,313
Give her back to me,

1708
01:14:43,347 --> 01:14:47,117
and I will give you the head
of your enemy.

1709
01:14:47,150 --> 01:14:49,987
- Alfredo Garcia?
- Enemy?

1710
01:14:50,020 --> 01:14:52,188
Nayland Smith.

1711
01:14:56,092 --> 01:14:57,260
Release him!

1712
01:14:57,293 --> 01:14:58,862
And let him love again.

1713
01:15:00,397 --> 01:15:01,765
Heel.

1714
01:15:05,635 --> 01:15:07,905
♪ Torn between two lovers ♪

1715
01:15:07,938 --> 01:15:09,205
♪ feeling like a-- ♪

1716
01:15:09,238 --> 01:15:10,873
Hmm?

1717
01:15:10,907 --> 01:15:13,877
My men are camped
in the hills

1718
01:15:13,910 --> 01:15:15,778
on the other side of--

1719
01:15:15,812 --> 01:15:16,880
he busts out a knife.

1720
01:15:16,913 --> 01:15:18,415
...overlooking this castle.

1721
01:15:18,448 --> 01:15:20,617
Nayland Smith
is their prisoner.

1722
01:15:20,650 --> 01:15:23,020
I want this woman.

1723
01:15:23,053 --> 01:15:25,622
Hey, who wouldn't?

1724
01:15:25,655 --> 01:15:27,925
She belongs to me.

1725
01:15:27,958 --> 01:15:29,693
I have the pink slip.

1726
01:15:29,727 --> 01:15:31,595
You shall have her,

1727
01:15:31,628 --> 01:15:34,632
provided that you give me
nayland Smith.

1728
01:15:34,665 --> 01:15:36,500
You have my word.

1729
01:15:36,533 --> 01:15:38,835
Then you shall
remain my guest.

1730
01:15:38,868 --> 01:15:41,505
For how long?

1731
01:15:41,538 --> 01:15:44,507
That, I shall decide.

1732
01:15:44,540 --> 01:15:47,077
You fiend,
you evil, diabolical,

1733
01:15:47,110 --> 01:15:49,378
treacherous, wonderful man!

1734
01:15:54,785 --> 01:15:56,920
[Humming casually]

1735
01:15:56,953 --> 01:15:58,488
Ed ames, you're not.

1736
01:15:59,623 --> 01:16:00,957
I was just testing the blade.

1737
01:16:00,990 --> 01:16:02,058
I hope you didn't take it
the wrong way!

1738
01:16:02,092 --> 01:16:03,660
The girl!

1739
01:16:03,693 --> 01:16:05,862
Let her go.
She cannot escape.

1740
01:16:05,895 --> 01:16:07,163
Not in those slacks.

1741
01:16:13,537 --> 01:16:15,105
Dad, can I borrow the car?

1742
01:16:20,310 --> 01:16:21,312
Uh-oh.

1743
01:16:21,345 --> 01:16:23,247
Take him to the laboratory.

1744
01:16:23,280 --> 01:16:25,882
Feed him to the clam.

1745
01:16:25,915 --> 01:16:28,918
Now, uh, where did I
park the...

1746
01:16:28,952 --> 01:16:30,853
Ohh.
[Scoffs]

1747
01:16:30,887 --> 01:16:32,722
The giraffe--
no.

1748
01:16:32,755 --> 01:16:35,358
[Chuckles]
She's really in the dark now.

1749
01:16:35,392 --> 01:16:36,627
Hmm...

1750
01:16:36,660 --> 01:16:39,562
Sting.
Stung.

1751
01:16:39,595 --> 01:16:41,798
Who timed this print,
Stevie wonder?

1752
01:16:41,832 --> 01:16:45,435
Whew.

1753
01:16:45,468 --> 01:16:49,773
Accepting the award
for editing...

1754
01:16:49,807 --> 01:16:52,642
It's "evening at the improv"
with fu manchu.

1755
01:16:52,675 --> 01:16:54,678
You are an obstinate man.

1756
01:16:54,712 --> 01:16:56,913
Thank you, sir.

1757
01:16:59,515 --> 01:17:01,885
How highly do you
value your life

1758
01:17:01,918 --> 01:17:03,620
compared with that
of nayland Smith?

1759
01:17:03,653 --> 01:17:04,955
Try me.

1760
01:17:04,988 --> 01:17:07,757
Ooh, let's crack
the party ball, man.

1761
01:17:07,790 --> 01:17:10,060
The entrance to eternity.

1762
01:17:10,093 --> 01:17:12,128
Oh, it's plainer than
I thought it would be, hmm.

1763
01:17:12,162 --> 01:17:14,965
...which leads directly
to the sea.

1764
01:17:14,998 --> 01:17:17,933
Cisterns of water are
poised above it.

1765
01:17:17,967 --> 01:17:19,302
A touch of the lever
will release

1766
01:17:19,335 --> 01:17:21,270
hundreds of thousands
of gallons of water...

1767
01:17:21,303 --> 01:17:22,838
Do it, do it.

1768
01:17:22,872 --> 01:17:25,475
And, combined with
professor heracles' crystals,

1769
01:17:25,508 --> 01:17:28,345
this can transform
the entire sea

1770
01:17:28,378 --> 01:17:31,648
into one gigantic
block of ice.

1771
01:17:31,682 --> 01:17:32,915
Which will be great
